we begin with the high stakes diplomacy  in geneva switzerland where u.s  president joe biden  and russia's president vladimir putin  have concluded their first  face-to-face summit both leaders have  described the nearly four hours of talks  as  being positive and cordial but there  remain areas of deep  division president putin denied that  moscow was responsible for recent cyber  attacks  on u.s institutions he also brushed  aside criticism of russia's human rights  record  including the jailing of opposition  leader alexei navalny  u.s president joe biden spoke about the  reason for this face-to-face meeting and  what he wanted  to make clear to president putin and  president putin and i  had a share unique responsibility  to manage the relationship between two  powerful and proud countries  a relationship that has to be stable  and predictable and it should be able to  we should be able to cooperate where  it's in our mutual interest  and where we have differences i want to  president putin understand  why i say what i say and why i do what i  do  and how we'll respond to specific to  specific kinds of actions  that harm america's interest just before  that  russian president vladimir putin was  asked what he thought about u.s  president joe biden  here's what the russian president had to  say  if you ask me what kind of a person and  interlocutor  president biden is i can say that he is  a constructive person  well balanced and experienced seasoned  politician and i expected that  he recalled his family  and conversations he had with his mother  well these things don't have  directly something to do with our  business but nevertheless  it shows his qualities and his moral  values  it is all appealing and i believe that  we spoke the same language  it doesn't necessarily mean that we need  to look into the eyes and see the soul  and to pledge eternal  love we defend the national interests  of our countries and this relationship  are always based on pragmatism  all right we want to go down to our very  own terry schultz she's covering this  summit for us in geneva switzerland good  evening to you  terry if if you look at the whole summit  this meeting which went almost four  hours i mean  how would you describe it can we say  that this was a successful  summit and if so for whom  i think we can brent because both  presidents came out of the meeting  telling  admittedly their sides of the stories  but similar versions of the story of the  topics that got discussed  uh they both said that they had  explained what their red lines were to  the other side  and that the other side had understood  that so that already is a  move forward in terms of transparency  president biden acknowledged that  you know some of the things they talked  about like moving ahead on  on possibly ending some of these cyber  attacks that we know  are backed by the kremlin he said he's  not sure that  that those will be stopped but that at  least talking about them raises that  possibility and one interesting thing he  said is that  is that he believes that president putin  is concerned about his credibility in  the world his standing in the world  and that possibly solving some of these  problems with the united states  might help that i want to ask you about  the red lines that  people have been talking about  particularly these cyber  attacks do we know after these talks  what would happen  if these cyber attacks in the united  states continue do we know what  president biden  plans to do as a response  i don't think we know specifically yet  he was pressed on that  in the news conference that took place  after the meeting he was asked what  exactly  would you do and he wouldn't he wouldn't  get into it specifically but he did  emphasize time and again that he had  made sure president putin knows there  would be a response  just something i would add from my life  covering nato brent is that  cyber is now an article 5 level offense  so  countries under nato have the right to  respond in a manner of their choosing to  cyber attacks and president putin knows  that well so  i would imagine that president biden may  have alluded to that when he was talking  about  what could happen if these cyber attacks  continue and  both of these presidents said that there  had been no threats made  during their talks but you have to  wonder  what what is the world going to do now  are we just going to wait and see  what happens are we going to wait and  see if but  putin does what biden asked him to do i  mean is that the mode that we're in now  i'm not sure but i think possibly there  will be a period now  of waiting to see if if the not  pledges so much but understandings that  that were brought about by this meeting  do take effect and i think cyber is one  of those areas where we will see very  quickly because there have been just  almost constant cyber attacks some of  them huge and very crippling on the  united states  and this is another interesting thing  president biden said he said that he  thought people in the room there in  their larger meeting  began to understand that this could very  much hurt russia directly  if these cyber attacks were to continue  if for example  when when you know fuel supplies are  harmed in the united states that will  definitely come around to affect moscow  as well  and we know that at summit's symbolism  is very  important we know that the white house  did not want there to be  a you know a shared or joint press  conference this time between the two  presidents as was the case back in 2018  in helsinki  you were in helsinki when that summit  took place between putin and then  president  trump um talk to me about the the  differences what struck you today  if you think about what it was like in  2018  well when president putin's press  conference went on so long you could  sort of understand why president biden  would not have been wanting to  stand there next to him and certainly  listen to president putin bring up  criticism of the united states and and  human rights in in the united states but  thinking back to helsinki that was  honestly a circus that press conference  i remember you had  uh president biden give uh give  president  trump a gift of of a ball which he  tossed out to  to the audience and everyone said oh my  gosh there's got to be a listening  device in that ball i mean there were  just  moments like this that were truly  literally jaw-dropping at that press  conference hearing president trump say  he trusted  uh putin over his own intelligence  agencies  nothing like that happened at this very  sober press conference today  and i don't think anyone could have  imagined president biden acting this way  yeah you have a very good memory though  i think it was a soccer ball that was  given to trump and i think the white  house  confirmed that it was checked for any  bucks terry schultz  in geneva terry thank you  and our coverage continues here at the  big table with our chief international  editor richard walker richard  what would you say what were the main  takeaways from this summit  yeah well i think i mean the contrast  couldn't be  clearer could it as as terry's example  shows  and i think really you have a sense of  sort of so far so good  you know the relationship between the us  and russia both sides did agree on this  that it was  pretty much at its lowest level since  the the end of the cold war and  it's hit some pretty deep lows during  that time  and the two leaders came out in their  separate con  press conferences really delivering  essentially quite similar messages about  the key agreements and the key steps  that had been agreed to there wasn't  like you know joe biden didn't come out  and say everything that vladimir putin  just said is nonsense he actually  indicated a couple of times in his press  conference that he'd been watching  vladimir putin and really didn't  contradict anything and now  there are steps which are taken in a  couple of areas particularly in this  area of strategic stability and we can  talk about that in a moment  um where they've started a dialogue and  they've  you know they've made some pretty bold  statements for instance  you know a joint presidential statement  from the kremlin and from the white  house saying that nuclear war cannot be  won  and must never be fought and kind of  recommitting the two sides  to this idea of arms control which is  something that has been really kind of  almost on its last legs the last few  years several arms controlled treaties  one side is either pulled out or they've  expired um  and the two sides saying actually no we  really kind of have to deal with this  stuff  and that's a change that is a change and  it's important to hear that  but at his press conference president  putin when asked about the cyber attacks  coming from russia targeting the u.s he  again denied  any responsibility for that so with that  the story hasn't changed one bit coming  from the kremlin yeah and that  i mean that's going to be one of the  tests of this year if the two sides are  recommitting to arms control  we're not in those old days of the you  know up to the 1980s and  you know some uh treaties which came  beyond that  where you're really saying okay big  nuclear weapons how many do you have of  which type  you know and try to get the numbers down  in the era of cyber war and other  high-tech  aspects of the military ai weapons  uh autonomous weapons stuff like that  you know where algorithms  you know uh the the other tools of the  weapons  you can't count those so you have to  come up with new ways of  of of dealing with these like how do you  come up with agreements about  you know what you cannot can and cannot  do what are your capabilities in the  cyber realm  so it's not going to be so much about  what you are capable of doing  it's what you choose to do and what you  agree to do or not do  but even that is difficult because in  the world of cyber you know  everything is deniable no it's not  absolutely  like cast iron you can say like a  missile it's just landed well i know  where that came from yeah  there's some psychology here too at play  um it seems like  president biden is giving is trying to  give putin we know this respect that  putin  wants so badly by suggesting  in in the press conference that he's the  president of russia  and he has the power and the authority  to control  you know his defenses and his weapons  and the cyber infrastructure  of his country so he's trying to give  something  to putin so that putin will also give  him the predictability of  saying there will be no more attacks  would you agree with that well yeah i  mean i think  biden was trying to kind of cast a new  level of respect  you know obama made the mis the mistake  of once referring to russia as a  regional power  and this was seen as you know this was  this was seen as a diplomatic mistake  you know this only made vladimir putin  want to prove his own power more  donald trump went in the opposite  direction he sort of flattered putin it  was almost bizarre  um what joe biden said in the early part  of his  uh press conference saying that you know  these two nations have a unique  responsibility because they have the  largest nuclear arsenals they both have  thousands of nuclear weapons even china  only has hundreds it is a totally  different level  and as proud nations it's their  responsibility to deal with that  that that will have been you know a  really carefully judged message  vladimir putin i'm showing you respect  you know the it was the  the whole event was a signal of respect  um  so biden will certainly be hoping that  this influences vladimir putin's um  behavior but biden himself said you know  don't watch  really what's happening now and this is  really good with the test what comes in  the next three to six months they're  setting up these working groups they're  setting up dialogues to look at these  things  do they actually come up with concrete  conclusions are they able to find areas  where they can say all right yeah we can  kind of agree on limits or  or patterns of behavior around these  things or does that begin to unravel  what happens in ukraine that's a dynamic  situation where you know  things could happen you know so there's  going to be an awful lot to monitor  really the next few months but  like i said at the beginning i think  kind of so far so good  all right our chief international editor  richard walker richard as always  thank you

